WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.430
When you think about Batman, your mind probably
goes to the grim avenger who stalks Gotham

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.020
City by night dishing out a darker brand of
justice, and you're not wrong.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.120
That's the image that’s made Batman one
of the most popular superheroes to ever hit

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:16.180
the comics page, and propelled him to success
in TV and movies as well.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.560
Since 1939, though, there have been thousands
of stories about Batman, and sometimes things

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.620
get pretty weird.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.200
From bizarre adventures to long-lost relatives,
there are more than a handful of things about

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.840
Batman that DC Comics would probably be happy
for readers to forget.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:31.640
The Dark Knockoff

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.240
After his debut in 1938, Superman's sudden
and overwhelming success prompted a massive

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.580
crowd of imitators, but Batman was different.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.100
The darker costume, the menacing demeanor,
and even the fact that he didn't have super-powers

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.440
helped him to stand out in the crowd, but
"unique" isn't really the right word for him.

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:51.960
Bill Finger and Bob Kane were pretty much
just ripping off the Shadow.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:57.100
The first Batman story, "The Case of the Chemical
Syndicate," appeared in Detective Comics #27

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.140
in 1939.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.579
But three years earlier, the Shadow, a pulp
hero known for his dark costume, and menacing

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.520
demeanor, starred in a novel called "Partners
of Peril."

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.880
To say that Batman's first adventure "borrowed"
from that story is the understatement of the

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:10.380
century.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.660
It's a beat-for-beat adaptation, right on
down to Batman's early penchant for guns and

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.380
his identity being revealed as a millionaire
socialite.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.560
The good news is that within a few months,
Finger and artists like Jerry Robinson and

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.720
Sheldon Moldoff had more fully separated the
Caped Crusader from his inspiration.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.020
And hey, at least he's not alone.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:32.330
Just ask Superman if he knows anything about
Clark "Doc" Savage and the arctic Fortress

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:34.700
of Solitude he had in 1938.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.540
My brother, my Deadman, and me

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.720
Way back in 1974’s World's Finest #223,
Bob Haney and Dick Dillin told a story so

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.460
weird that even Batman himself referred to
it as "the most stunning, shocking thing that

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.990
ever happened in my whole life…which has
not exactly been dull."

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.800
In a team-up with Superman and the ghostly
Deadman, Batman followed the trail of a murderer

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.490
who killed his victims with gigantic razor-sharp
boomerangs, and discovered that the man he

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.800
was hunting was actually the brother he never
knew.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.090
It seems his big bro Thomas Wayne Jr. had
been driven insane by a car accident from

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.310
when he was a baby.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.029
He learned how to make boomerangs as part
of his therapy, and was then manipulated into

00:02:14.029 --> 00:02:15.920
a very complicated murder spree.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.940
And that's not even the weird part.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.499
That happens at the end of the story, when
Deadman, a ghostly superhero with the ability

00:02:22.499 --> 00:02:27.569
to possess the living, just decides to take
over Thomas' body as his new flesh-and-blood

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:29.200
home, without ever telling Batman.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.579
Instead, he just assumes that his "buddy”
Batman would be cool with it, and wanders

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.960
off to have his own adventures.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.000
Needless to say, while we would eventually
see more of Thomas Jr., no other creators

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.460
ever referenced this particular story again.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.480
Alfred goes bad

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.700
Speaking of weird things that have happened
to the Batman family, Alfred's brief tenure

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:53.090
as a telekinetic crime boss who looked like
a humanoid golf ball is also something that

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.680
we don't really talk about anymore.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.920
In 1964, Jack Schiff and Sheldon Moldoff seemingly
killed off Batman's butler Alfred, having

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.680
him sacrifice his life to save the Dynamic
Duo.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.240
Six months later, though, Batman faced a new
foe: The Outsider.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.880
For almost two years, the Outsider challenged
Batman and Robin, acting as a mastermind who

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.260
manipulated other villains.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.020
And when they finally confronted him, he was
revealed to be Alfred.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.980
It turned out their faithful butler had been
resurrected by a scientist whose "regeneration

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.640
machine" turned him into a mutant who hated
his former employers.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.900
Fortunately, the process turned out to be
extremely easy to reverse, and before long,

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.640
he was back to dusting the Batcave.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:34.700
And believe it or not, this one actually did
get referenced again later on.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.840
Nocturna’s custody battle

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.019
If you’re a casual Batman fan, you might
think that he only did weird stuff back in

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.960
the long-ago days of the Silver Age.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.979
Once the ‘80s rolled around with Dark Knight
Returns and Death in the Family, things got

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.280
dark and serious, right?

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:53.160
Sure, as long as “dark and serious” includes
a vampire taking Batman to court to get custody

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:53.860
of Robin.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.150
It happened in 1983 with Natalia Knight, who
was about as close as you could get to a vampire

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:01.740
without actually having someone get bitten
on the neck.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.880
While working as an astronomer, she was zapped
by a weird ray that drained her skin of pigmentation

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:11.409
and left her super-sensitive to light, so
naturally, she decided to become a supervillain

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:12.750
named Nocturna.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:13.250
Like you do.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:18.280
She also fell into a love quadrangle with
Vicki Vale and Alfred's daughter, Julia, who

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:20.420
were all vying for Bruce Wayne's affections.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.710
The thing is, rather than long walks on the
beach, Nocturna's strategy involved waiting

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:29.500
for Bruce to adopt a new ward, Jason Todd,
and then adopting him first so that Bruce

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.420
would have to marry her if he really wanted
to be a Bat-dad.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:33.860
The weirdest part?

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:34.680
It almost worked.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:40.180
Jason actually said in court that he'd prefer
to be adopted by Natalia, only to reveal later

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.720
that it had all been part of an ill-fated
attempt at going undercover.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:48.520
Eventually, Nocturna was seemingly killed
in a hot air balloon crash, no, really, and

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.380
that was the end of the world's most complicated
marriage proposal.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:52.960
Electric Death

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.380
When you think about Batman's greatest enemies,
the names that usually come to mind are the

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:58.140
big ones.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:58.960
The Joker.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.360
Ra's al-Ghul.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:04.240
You can even make a pretty decent argument
for the Riddler, if you really put your mind

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:04.740
to it.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:09.320
But if you're going by who actually had the
most success at the goal of actually killing

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.140
Batman, then the Dark Knight's greatest foe
is actually a guy named Lester Buchinsky:

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:14.600
the Electrocutioner!

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.480
Yeah, it’s hard to believe, especially since
he technically killed Batman by accident.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.080
The Electrocutioner was another vigilante
who figured that if you couldn't bring the

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:26.020
criminals to the electric chair, you might
as well bring the electric chair to them.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:31.200
In a 1992 story from Detective Comics, he
and Batman were on the trail of the same criminal.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.099
When the Electrocutioner zapped him with a
shock big enough to stop his heart, Batman

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.240
was technically dead for a good minute and
a half.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.120
The only reason that he didn't stay that way
was that Robin was there, and had the good

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.060
sense to wear an insulated costume.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:47.540
While the Electrocutioner originally just
shrugged off literally killing the Batman,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.129
Robin eventually beat enough sense into him
that he agreed to use his electric gloves

00:05:51.129 --> 00:05:53.800
as a defibrillator to shock Batman back to
life.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.320
And then they both presumably agreed to never
again speak of the time Batman almost died

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:02.620
in a sewer at the hands of a D-List schlub
that had to be beaten into compliance by a

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.440
spiky haired teenager with a stick.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.560
The Dark Knockoff Returns

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.260
With Batman’s popularity, it's no surprise
that Gotham City would become a pretty important

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:12.780
place in the DC Universe.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:16.960
The last 75 years have seen a history develop
for the Dark Knight's hometown that's also

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.320
seen it serve as the home of other superheroes
from throughout the timeline of the DC Universe.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.660
The Golden Age Green Lantern, a violent vigilante
called the Reaper who was around in the years

00:06:25.669 --> 00:06:30.090
before Bruce Wayne's parents were killed,
and even cowboy antihero Jonah Hex, who was

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.400
hanging around there back in the 1800s, and
also in the future.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:34.460
It’s complicated.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.480
Of course, it's one thing if Batman's not
the first superhero to make his home in Gotham

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:38.980
City.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:43.720
But if he's not actually the first Gotham
superhero to dress up as a bat, well…that's

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:44.560
a little weird.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:49.900
In 1998, an unexpected tale in the pages of
Chase, a short-lived but underrated comic

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:54.139
series, revealed that Cameron Chase's distrust
of masked avengers came from the fact that

00:06:54.139 --> 00:06:59.400
her father was one: the Acro-Bat, who fought
crime in the ‘70s alongside an obscure and

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.360
hilariously named team called the Justice
Experience.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.060
Unsurprisingly, his story doesn't end well,
but once you know he was out there, it's hard

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.720
to not think that Bruce Wayne was biting his
style.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.720
The Corpse that Wouldn’t Die!

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:15.260
The Electrocutioner wasn't the only one to
score a (technical) kill on Batman.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:20.210
In The Brave and the Bold #115, however, the
culprit was even less impressive: a booby-trapped

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.820
rain-spout rigged up by a couple of completely
average criminals.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.620
The shock did him in, but thanks to Batman's
"iron constitution" keeping his heart beating,

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.980
he's only mostly dead.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:34.540
Fortunately, the Atom gets the bright idea
to solve the Batman's "final" case by shrinking

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.990
down and hopping around on his brain in hopes
that this will stimulate his crime-fighting

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.980
instincts and allow him to mindlessly do things
like punch out crooks and rescue a kidnapped

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:44.480
girl.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:45.480
And it actually works.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:49.760
It turns out that having a little tiny man
hop on your brain is enough stimulus to basically

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.500
jumpstart everything and get you back in good
working order.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:56.159
It might not be that great for the legend
of the Batman to have him die at random by

00:07:56.159 --> 00:08:00.600
jumping at the wrong window, but that's balanced
out by the fact that death itself cannot stop

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:04.449
him from getting up off a slab in the morgue
and busting criminals' jaws all over Gotham

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:05.349
City.

